        <dataout:penaltyCategory rdf:resource="https://dataout.org/ontology#fine"/>
        <dataout:penaltyValue>
          <schema:MonetaryAmount rdf:nodeID="nb339817b2c8941f983f86810fb398ca2b49">
            <schema:currency>RUB</schema:currency>
            <schema:value rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">150000</schema:value>
          </schema:MonetaryAmount>
        </dataout:penaltyValue>
      </dataout:Penalty>
    </dataout:hasPenalty>
    <dataout:hasSnippet xml:lang="en">A German citizen was fined and ordered to leave the country for corresponding with a man on the gay dating app “Hornet”. The court found that the defendant's private messages “promoted non-traditional sexual attitudes, the attractiveness of non-traditional sexual relations, and a distorted idea of the social equivalence of traditional and non-traditional sexual relations”, and that the defendant “exerted ideological and psychological influence on [the message recipient], which led to the imposition of a system of ideas and values alien and destructive to Russian society.” The judge cited three messages the defendant sent to another “Hornet” user: “Being gay is allowed”; “It is simply sad that we have to separate people depending on what they do in bed… this should be a private matter”; “In the West, they are all so absorbed in themselves that they do not actually give their partner emotions and passion in bed.” The user with whom the defendant corresponded was a witness in this case, stating in his testimony that he responded to the defendant that “in Russia, LGBT is prohibited both morally and legally.”</dataout:hasSnippet>
