        <dataout:penaltyValue>
          <schema:MonetaryAmount rdf:nodeID="nb339817b2c8941f983f86810fb398ca2b36">
            <schema:currency>RUB</schema:currency>
            <schema:value rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">500000</schema:value>
          </schema:MonetaryAmount>
        </dataout:penaltyValue>
      </dataout:Penalty>
    </dataout:hasPenalty>
    <dataout:hasSnippet xml:lang="en">“Universal Communications” was fined after the court found that its television channel “Hollywood” had broadcast two films — “The Infiltrator” (2016, Brad Furman) and “Official Competition” (2021, Mariano Cohn and Gastón Duprat) — to minors under a 16+ age rating. In relation to “The Infiltrator”, the court isolated a fragment stating that it contained “visual information corresponding to signs of the demonstration of non-traditional sexual relations or preferences.” For “Official Competition”, the judgment focused on a scene described as “a prolonged kiss between two persons of the same biological sex (female), expressing sexual feelings,” with the court adding that “other sexual actions are also demonstrated.” It then extended the analysis by classifying kisses into social and sensual categories, concluding that the scene belonged to the latter because it conveyed affection, tenderness, and love rather than neutral social contact. These quoted observations formed the basis for finding that both films demonstrated non-traditional sexual relations and therefore could not lawfully remain accessible to minors under a 16+ classification.</dataout:hasSnippet>
